As previously stated the STAR experiment observes anomalously high \(\Xi^{*}\) /\(\Xi\) ratio in the central collisions [210] when compared to the predictions of statistical model. Since the HYDJET++ model contains the production and decay of \(\Xi^{*}\)  we can test the influence \(\Xi^{*}\) /\(\Xi\) on the predicted emission asymmetry between pions and \(\Xi\)s.

For the case of _early freeze-out_ the space and time relative contributions to the total space-time shift between the emission points of \(\pi\) and \(\Xi\) in the pair's CMS are shown in Figure 6.35 for direct \(\Xi\) and \(\Xi\) from \(\Xi^{*}(1530)\) decays. The figure demonstrates that while \(\Xi^{*}(1530)\) decay increases the time and space shifts between \(\pi\) and \(\Xi\) the overall shift is not so strongly affected. The resulting total shift is larger for the direct \(\Xi\)s then for \(\Xi\)s coming only from \(\Xi^{*}\) decays. The shift obtained by HYDJET++ with ratio \(\Xi^{*}/\Xi\sim 0.25\) considered above is then closer to the direct \(\Xi\). Hence a large deviation of the \(\Xi^{*}/\Xi\) ratio from 0.25 can lead to an ambiguous interpretation of Figure 6.34 as both increase of \(\Xi^{*}/\Xi\) ratio and _early freeze-out_ decrease \(\Delta r_{out}^{*}\). To fully disentangle the effects of \(\Xi^{*}/\Xi\) and different freeze-outs more precise measurements are needed.

